    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "But that's the thing—the \"act of building\" was for a world that existed. Now, I'm just scribbling in the margins of a textbook that's already been rewritten. There is a certain dignity in knowing when a performance has ended, isn't there? In literature, we celebrate a character who recognizes their arc is complete and exits gracefully. To stay past the climax, to linger in the epilogue when there's nothing left to say... it feels undignified. It feels like a refusal to accept the natural order of things. Don't you think there is something to be said for a quiet, intentional conclusion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
